<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kalka is a town in the Panchkula district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryana">Haryana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the foot of the Shivalik Hills. It is the starting point of the narrow-gauge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalka-shimla-railway">Kalka–Shimla Railway</a>, opened in 1903, and of the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, the summer capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, which made it the gateway to the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of the Simla Hills.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalka.jpg" alt="Kalka" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Billjones94 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalka_Temple_in_Panchkula,_in_Haryana,_photographed_by_Yogabrata_Chakraborty,_on_June_4,_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name of the town comes from Kalika Maa, the goddess Kali, its ruling deity. The central part of the town was established by Pundir Rajputs, and the area was under them and governed by local Rajput zamindars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> acquired Kalka from the princely state of Patiala in 1843 as a stopover and depot for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a>, the summer capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>. In 1846 it was transferred to Shimla district and in 1899 to Ambala district.</p><p>In the 1800s the British took the land from the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> for railway development, which gave the town an economic boost. It became the junction of the Delhi-Panipat-Ambala-Kalka railway line and the Kalka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a> railway, which was begun in 1898 and opened to traffic in 1903. By 1901 Kalka, administered as a notified area, had a population of 7,045, a railway workshop and a market for ginger and turmeric. Its municipal committee was created on 11 April 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kalka lies on National Highway 5 between Chandigarh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>, near Solan, and is the gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>. Pinjore is to its south and the industrial village of Parwanoo to its north. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2011 counted 34,314 people in Kalka and 86,550 in the urban area made up of Kalka, Pinjore and Parwanoo. The local language is Baghati, though Punjabi, Haryanvi and Hindi are also used. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the main religion, followed by 81.54 per cent of the population, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> the second, at 13.02 per cent.</p><p>Kalka railway station is the northern terminus of the Delhi-Kalka line, with broad gauge towards Chandigarh and narrow gauge towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Shimla</a>. The Kalka-Shimla Railway is on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage list. The ancient temple of Kali on National Highway 22 draws worshippers during Navratri, and a fair is held there each year in March and April. Oral tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> built the original temple during their exile. Nearby is the Chandimandir Cantonment, home of the Western Command of the Indian army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baghat was a small hill state on the road from <b>Kalka</b> to Shimla.</blockquote><a href="/state/baghat-state">Baghat State</a></li></ul></section>
